# gBV_JTt3Kxk-split-flange-coaster — the gBV minimal coaster in two halves, its pieces held by a flange in an undercut.
#
# Split option C (3d-models split-with-studs design §11, call 6): the coaster is
# cut flat through its thickness at `half`, into a lower and an upper half. Each
# hole keeps its own shape at the two faces and widens into the straps near the
# cut, an undercut `flange` mm deep into the strap on each side. A loose piece is
# stepped to match: its own shape at both faces, and a flange near its middle that
# sits in the undercut. Dropped into the lower half before the upper half goes on,
# it cannot fall out of either face, and each face shows its whole outline, flush.
#
#   --piece Lower   the lower half, face down
#   --piece Upper   the upper half, turned over to print face down
#   --piece Middle | Kite | Hex | Star | Outer   one ring of pieces, packed to print
#
# The coaster's own name renders the closed coaster with every piece in place.
# That render is a picture, not a print: its halves and pieces touch. So
# `--check` on the whole file gates each half and each piece color on its own,
# the way `--piece` does. Print each --piece on its own.
#
# Alignment pegs sit at strap junctions only. Along a strap, the rib between two
# undercuts is the strap less two flanges wide, too narrow for a peg and its
# socket wall at 1.25 times; where straps meet there is room, and the split
# kernel measures each site from the undercut, so it puts them there. split-02
# (3d-models) printed with no pegs, and its halves came apart in the hand. The
# widest junction at size 112.5 has 1.920 mm of room (measured 2026-10-22, the
# same at piece gap 0.2 and 0.25), and a peg needs half its diameter plus half
# its gap plus 0.9 mm of wall: a 2 mm peg needs 1.925 at gap 0.05 and does not
# fit, and a 1.7 mm peg fits up to gap 0.3. Smaller coasters have less: the
# widest junction is 1.615 mm at size 100, 1.357 at 90 and 1.101 at 80, too
# little for any useful peg. So `stud` defaults to 0, no pegs, as `id` does,
# and the plate that prints it at 112.5 sets it (split-03: 1.7).
#
# Pin rings and bosses (2026-10-23, 3d-models split-06: "more pins around central
# star", "too many pins around border"). A ring puts its pegs evenly round a
# circle, the first at its turn, each moved to the roomiest spot within 1.5 mm;
# a peg with no room there is refused by name. With `stud_boss 1` the undercut
# is filled in a disc round each peg, so a peg needs room only from the hole at
# the face: round the middle star, where the undercuts leave no junction room, a
# ring of ten 1.2 mm pegs then fits. The rings and the boss are all 0 by default,
# which builds exactly as before.
#
# `room` shortens each piece by that much, so the halves close on each other and
# not on a piece. split-02's halves, with pieces of the full height, closed with
# a space between them; pieces as tall as the gap between the halves' floors
# may be why, which a print with `room` above 0 tests.
#
# The defaults are 1.25 times the minimal coaster (sheets-04d, 3d-models): size
# 112.5, strap 3.75, height 4.4. The strap follows the size, so `size` alone
# scales the coaster the way sheets-04d did:
#
#   bikar render patterns/Constructions/gBV_JTt3Kxk-split-flange-coaster.bkr --format stl --check \
#     --piece Lower -o out/lower.stl
#
# The construction below is patterns/Constructions/gBV_JTt3Kxk-minimal-pieces.bkr
# from its blueprint to the end of its pattern, copied unchanged.

param size = 112.5 range 80..125   # mm, the coaster's span across the strap network; 112.5 is the 1.25x coaster we print
param strap = $size / 30   # mm, strap width, scaled with size as the minimal coaster is: 3 at 90, 3.75 at 112.5
param height = 4.4 range 3.2..6   # mm, the closed coaster's height; each half is half of it
param half = $height / 2   # mm, where the coaster is cut: half its height
param flange = 0.8 range 0.8..1.2   # mm, how far the undercut reaches into the strap on each side; the piece's flange is as wide less the gap
param gap = 0.25 range 0..0.5   # mm per face between a piece and its pocket; loose enough to drop in (CAL-LSE-01)
param spacing = 2 range 1..10   # mm, the least gap between two pieces of a ring on the bed (`pack zipper spacing`)
param room = 0 range 0..0.6   # mm, how much shorter than the closed coaster each piece is, so the halves meet each other; 0 is the full height split-02 printed
param halves = 0 range 0..1 step 1   # 1 prints each loose piece as two halves cut at its middle, each on one of its faces, so both faces print smooth on the bed (3d-models cut-pieces-joins design, D-120); the pins or the slide below hold the two halves together
param slope = 0 range 0..0.4 step 0.1   # mm, stairs under each piece's flange, each this much out from the boss per this much up, so the step out prints as a 45° slope at that layer height and not a ledge over air; 0 is the ledge split-07 printed, whose stars' faces curved (3d-models split-10, 2026-10-27). A piece with a slide is refused it
param pin = 0 range 0..2   # mm, with halves: the diameter of the pins that join each piece's two halves, 0 for none; each pin stands on the lower half and goes into a hole in the upper (3d-models cut-pieces-joins design, step 2)
param pin_gap = 0.25 range 0..0.5 step 0.05   # mm, how much wider a pin's hole is than the pin, across; 0.25 is split-06's stud gap, the same pin, nozzle and filament
param pin_wall = 0.9 range 0.4..1.5 step 0.05   # mm, the band kept round each pin's hole; 0.9 is two lines
param pin_spacing = 0 range 0..20   # mm, the least distance between two pins on one piece; 0 is the least that fits, the pin, its gap and one wall
param pins_middle = 0 range 0..7 step 1   # how many pins join each half of the middle piece (orbit 0); three or more stand one in the middle and the rest evenly round it near the edge, so 5 is a middle and four round, 7 a middle and six
param pins_kite = 0 range 0..6 step 1   # the same for each kite (orbit 1); a kite has room for one at 1.2 mm
param pins_hex = 0 range 0..6 step 1   # the same for each hexagon (orbit 2)
param pins_star = min(3, 30 * $pin) range 0..6 step 1   # the same for each star (orbit 3), but 3 by default whenever there is a pin size, on a triangle round its middle (pins_star_ring below), and none with pin 0, so halves with no pins still build: 3d-models star-2 (2026-11-02) printed the triangle at 1.2, 1.0 and 0.8 mm, and Omar kept the 1.2 mm one ("left most was best"; the 0.8 mm pins broke and turned). Pins show only with halves 1. Three 1.2 mm pins with the 0.9 mm wall fit the star at the piece gap the plates print, 0.1, with or without a slope; at this file's gap default, 0.25, the star is too small and they are refused by name (measured 2026-11-03)
param pins_outer = 0 range 0..6 step 1   # the same for each outer piece (orbit 4)
param pins_star_ring = min(1, max(0, $pins_star - 1)) range 0..1 step 1   # 1 stands each star's pins evenly on one circle round its middle with none in it, so 3 make a triangle centred on the star (3d-models star-1); by default 1 for two or more pins and 0 for one, so a recipe that asks for one star pin keeps it in the middle, as split-07, split-09, split-10, hexfit-1 and star-1 printed it
param slide = 0 range 0..6   # mm, with halves: the width at its root of a dovetail rail on each lower half's cut face, which slides into a slot in the upper half, 0 for none; the rail runs across the face, the slot edge to edge (3d-models cut-pieces-joins design, step 3; sld-1's rail is 4)
param slide_gap = 0.01 range -0.1..0.3 step 0.01   # mm per side between rail and slot; sld-1's 0.10, 0.15 and 0.20 all slid and held (D-120), but on this coaster's hexagons split-10 found 0.15 to 0.05 all wiggled, and hexfit-1 tried 0.15 down to -0.04: Omar, 2026-10-29, "4 might have been the best, 1 too loose, 6 too tight", so 0.01 (4 dots) over 0.15 (1) and -0.02 (6), tried on the hexagon with a 4 mm rail 0.8 tall and the slot one layer deeper (3d-models hexfit-1). Under 0 the slot is that much narrower than the rail, a press fit, down to -0.1 (CAL-FIT-01's press step)
param slide_height = 0.8 range 0.4..1.2 step 0.2   # mm, the rail's height in whole 0.2 mm layers; the slot is slide_slot_extra layers deeper, and 0.5 mm of band must be left under it where it runs out past the face
param slide_slot_extra = 1 range 0..1 step 1   # whole 0.2 mm layers the slot runs on past the rail's top: 1 (sld-1's) leaves air over the rail so the cut faces close flat, 0 makes the slot exactly as deep as the rail
param slide_middle = 0 range 0..1 step 1   # 1 joins each half of the middle piece (orbit 0) with the slide; a face that turns inward is refused
param slide_kite = 0 range 0..1 step 1   # the same for each kite (orbit 1)
param slide_hex = 0 range 0..1 step 1   # the same for each hexagon (orbit 2)
param slide_star = 0 range 0..1 step 1   # the same for each star (orbit 3)
param slide_outer = 0 range 0..1 step 1   # the same for each outer piece (orbit 4)
param dots_middle = 0 range 0..8 step 1   # with halves: how many dots are sunk into each half's cut face of the middle piece (orbit 0), hidden once the halves close, so an experiment plate's versions of a piece can be told apart off the bed (3d-models split-10, 2026-10-27); 0 for none, and past 6 in two rows
param dots_kite = 0 range 0..8 step 1   # the same for each kite (orbit 1)
param dots_hex = 0 range 0..8 step 1   # the same for each hexagon (orbit 2)
param dots_star = 0 range 0..8 step 1   # the same for each star (orbit 3)
param dots_outer = 0 range 0..8 step 1   # the same for each outer piece (orbit 4)
param dots_bottom = 0 range 0..1 step 1   # 1 puts every piece's dots into its lower half's bed face (the face it prints on) instead of both cut faces, so they leave the cut face to the slide (3d-models hex fit plate, 2026-10-28); 0 keeps them on the cut faces
param stud = 0 range 0..1.9   # mm, the alignment peg's diameter, 0 for none; 1.7 at size 112.5, where a 2 mm peg has no junction with room for its socket, and none below that (see the note above). 1.9 fits at 112.5 only up to gap 0.05
param stud_gap = 0.05 range -0.1..0.5 step 0.05   # mm, how much wider a socket is than its peg, across; 0.05 is the snug fit the split kernel takes when none is written (CAL-FIT-01). SPL-1 printed every rung to 0.15 too tight to close by hand, so the range runs on to 0.5
param stud_spacing = 25 range 3..60   # mm, the least distance between two pegs; the kernel puts them only at strap junctions here, where the undercuts leave room for a socket
param stud_wall = 0.9 range 0.4..1.5 step 0.05   # mm, the wall kept round each socket on the cut face; it sets the disc round each peg, so how far it bites each piece's flange. 0.9 is two lines, what split-06 printed; 0.4, one line, is the least the kernel takes
param stud_ring1 = 0 range 0..60   # mm, the radius of a ring of pegs round the centre; 0 for none. Any ring turns off the automatic pick, so every peg is one a ring names (see the note on pin rings above)
param stud_ring1_n = 0 range 0..20 step 1   # how many pegs stand evenly round ring 1
param stud_ring1_turn = 0 range -180..180   # degrees counterclockwise from +x where ring 1's first peg stands
param stud_ring2 = 0 range 0..60   # mm, a second ring of pegs, as ring 1
param stud_ring2_n = 0 range 0..20 step 1
param stud_ring2_turn = 0 range -180..180
param stud_ring3 = 0 range 0..60   # mm, a third ring of pegs, as ring 1
param stud_ring3_n = 0 range 0..20 step 1
param stud_ring3_turn = 0 range -180..180
param stud_ring4 = 0 range 0..60   # mm, a fourth ring of pegs, as ring 1
param stud_ring4_n = 0 range 0..20 step 1
param stud_ring4_turn = 0 range -180..180
param stud_ring5 = 0 range 0..60   # mm, a fifth ring of pegs, as ring 1
param stud_ring5_n = 0 range 0..20 step 1
param stud_ring5_turn = 0 range -180..180
param stud_boss = 0 range 0..1 step 1   # 1 fills the undercut in a disc round each peg in both halves, and notches each piece's flange to match, so a peg needs room only from the hole at the face, not from the undercut
param wedge = 360 range 36..360   # degrees, print only the faces whose centres lie in this angle, a test slice of both halves with whole pegs and sockets; 360 is the whole coaster. The range starts at 36, two face centres' worth, as on the lip coaster
param wedge_from = 9 range 0..359   # degrees counterclockwise from +x where the wedge starts; every face centre sits on a multiple of 18 degrees, so 9 puts the edges halfway between them
param wedge_center = 0 range 0..1   # 1 also keeps the middle piece and its opening in the wedge; the middle sits on the coaster's centre, so no angle holds it otherwise (3d-models split-04, a fit test of every piece kind)
param id = 0 range 0..9 step 1   # the prototype's id, 0 for none, cut into the lower half's bottom so two closed coasters can be told apart (3d-models feedback page 2026-10-06: "every prototype should have an id"); 1 to 9 but 4, which is refused: a 4's hole closes up under a 2.4 mm cap, and this bottom has room only at 2 mm; measured at size 112.5, while 80, 90 and 100 leave no room for any id, so none by default, so the plate that prints it sets it
param unit = ($size - $strap) / 2.0001   # mm per GeoGebra unit — the art spans K units; the strap adds strap/2 each side

blueprint gBV_JTt3Kxk_scaffold
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # root frame — A = centre, B/D = unit-circle divisions (D-061)
  circle unit center(0, 0) radius $unit
  divide unit into 4
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # O = (0, 0)
  point O = unit.mpt
  # E = (1, 0)
  point E = unit.cpt0
  # W = (-1, 0)
  point W = unit.cpt2
  # h = Line(W, E)
  line h from W to E
  # c = Circle(O, E)
  circle c center(O) through E
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # t=00:10  cL = Circle(W, O)
  circle cL center(W) through O
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # cR = Circle(E, O)
  circle cR center(E) through O
  # bis = PerpendicularBisector(O, E)
  bisector bis from O to E
  # K1 = Rotate(W, -66°, E)
  point K1 = rotate W by -66 around E
  # K2 = Rotate(W, -54°, E)
  point K2 = rotate W by -54 around E
  # K3 = Rotate(E, 54°, W)
  point K3 = rotate E by 54 around W
  # K4 = Rotate(E, 66°, W)
  point K4 = rotate E by 66 around W
  # K5 = Rotate(W, 54°, E)
  point K5 = rotate W by 54 around E
  # K6 = Rotate(W, 66°, E)
  point K6 = rotate W by 66 around E
  # K7 = Rotate(E, -66°, W)
  point K7 = rotate E by -66 around W
  # K8 = Rotate(E, -54°, W)
  point K8 = rotate E by -54 around W
  # tick1 = CircularArc(E, K1, K2)
  circle tick1_host center(E) through K1
  # tick2 = CircularArc(W, K3, K4)
  circle tick2_host center(W) through K3
  # tick3 = CircularArc(E, K5, K6)
  circle tick3_host center(E) through K5
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # t=00:15  tick4 = CircularArc(W, K7, K8)
  circle tick4_host center(W) through K7
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # v = PerpendicularBisector(W, E)
  bisector v from W to E
  # M = Midpoint(O, E)
  point M = midpoint O E
  # V0 = Rotate(E, 90°, O)
  point V0 = rotate E by 90 around O
  # cm = Circle(M, V0)
  circle cm center(M) through V0
  # rW = Line(M, W)
  line rW from M to W
  # Gd = Intersect(cm, rW, W)
  point Gd = intersect cm rW pick nearest W
  # SM = Rotate(V0, -5°, M)
  point SM = rotate V0 by -5 around M
  # TM = Rotate(Gd, 3°, M)
  point TM = rotate Gd by 3 around M
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # t=00:20  arcM = CircularArc(M, SM, TM)
  circle arcM_host center(M) through SM
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # V2 = Rotate(V0, 72°, O)
  point V2 = rotate V0 by 72 around O
  # V8 = Rotate(V0, -72°, O)
  point V8 = rotate V0 by -72 around O
  # V4 = Rotate(V2, 72°, O)
  point V4 = rotate V2 by 72 around O
  # V6 = Rotate(V8, -72°, O)
  point V6 = rotate V8 by -72 around O
  # S0 = Rotate(V2, -5°, V0)
  point S0 = rotate V2 by -5 around V0
  # T0 = Rotate(V8, 7°, V0)
  point T0 = rotate V8 by 7 around V0
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # t=00:25  arc0 = CircularArc(V0, S0, T0)
  circle arc0_host center(V0) through S0
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # S2 = Rotate(V4, -18°, V2)
  point S2 = rotate V4 by -18 around V2
  # T2 = Rotate(V0, 6°, V2)
  point T2 = rotate V0 by 6 around V2
  # arc2 = CircularArc(V2, S2, T2)
  circle arc2_host center(V2) through S2
  # S8 = Rotate(V0, -16°, V8)
  point S8 = rotate V0 by -16 around V8
  # T8 = Rotate(V6, 6°, V8)
  point T8 = rotate V6 by 6 around V8
  # arc8 = CircularArc(V8, S8, T8)
  circle arc8_host center(V8) through S8
  # S4 = Rotate(V6, -17°, V4)
  point S4 = rotate V6 by -17 around V4
  # T4 = Rotate(V2, 12°, V4)
  point T4 = rotate V2 by 12 around V4
  # arc4 = CircularArc(V4, S4, T4)
  circle arc4_host center(V4) through S4
  # S6 = Rotate(V8, -12°, V6)
  point S6 = rotate V8 by -12 around V6
  # T6 = Rotate(V4, 17°, V6)
  point T6 = rotate V4 by 17 around V6
  # arc6 = CircularArc(V6, S6, T6)
  circle arc6_host center(V6) through S6
  # V5 = Reflect(V0, O)
  point V5 = rotate V0 by 180 around O
  # V7 = Reflect(V2, O)
  point V7 = rotate V2 by 180 around O
  # V9 = Reflect(V4, O)
  point V9 = rotate V4 by 180 around O
  # V1 = Reflect(V6, O)
  point V1 = rotate V6 by 180 around O
  # V3 = Reflect(V8, O)
  point V3 = rotate V8 by 180 around O
  # ln18 = Line(V3, V8)
  line ln18 from V3 to V8
  # ln54 = Line(V4, V9)
  line ln54 from V4 to V9
  # ln126 = Line(V6, V1)
  line ln126 from V6 to V1
  # ln162 = Line(V7, V2)
  line ln162 from V7 to V2
  # cD1 = Circle(O, 2.29)
  circle cD1 center(O) radius 2.29 * $unit
  # cD2 = Circle(O, 2.22)
  circle cD2 center(O) radius 2.22 * $unit
  # cD3 = Circle(O, 2.13)
  circle cD3 center(O) radius 2.13 * $unit
  # cD4 = Circle(O, 2.37)
  circle cD4 center(O) radius 2.37 * $unit
  # cD5 = Circle(O, 2.19)
  circle cD5 center(O) radius 2.19 * $unit
  # cD6 = Circle(O, 2.32)
  circle cD6 center(O) radius 2.32 * $unit
  # cD7 = Circle(O, 2.27)
  circle cD7 center(O) radius 2.27 * $unit
  # cD8 = Circle(O, 2.24)
  circle cD8 center(O) radius 2.24 * $unit
  # D1 = Intersect(cD1, ln18, V8)
  point D1 = intersect cD1 ln18 pick nearest V8
  # D2 = Intersect(cD2, ln18, V3)
  point D2 = intersect cD2 ln18 pick nearest V3
  # D3 = Intersect(cD3, ln54, V9)
  point D3 = intersect cD3 ln54 pick nearest V9
  # D4 = Intersect(cD4, ln54, V4)
  point D4 = intersect cD4 ln54 pick nearest V4
  # D5 = Intersect(cD5, ln126, V1)
  point D5 = intersect cD5 ln126 pick nearest V1
  # D6 = Intersect(cD6, ln126, V6)
  point D6 = intersect cD6 ln126 pick nearest V6
  # D7 = Intersect(cD7, ln162, V2)
  point D7 = intersect cD7 ln162 pick nearest V2
  # D8 = Intersect(cD8, ln162, V7)
  point D8 = intersect cD8 ln162 pick nearest V7
  # d18 = Segment(D2, D1)
  segment d18 from D2 to D1
  # d54 = Segment(D4, D3)
  segment d54 from D4 to D3
  # d126 = Segment(D6, D5)
  segment d126 from D6 to D5
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # t=00:38  d162 = Segment(D8, D7)
  segment d162 from D8 to D7
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # blue1 = Polygon(V0, V2, V4, V6, V8)
  polygon blue1 [ V0 V2 V4 V6 V8 ]
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # t=00:45  blue2 = Polygon(V1, V3, V5, V7, V9)
  polygon blue2 [ V1 V3 V5 V7 V9 ]
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # t=00:52  pink = Polygon(V0, V7, V4, V1, V8, V5, V2, V9, V6, V3)
  polygon pink [ V0 V7 V4 V1 V8 V5 V2 V9 V6 V3 ]
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # eb = Line(V9, V1)
  line eb from V9 to V1
  # ep = Line(V0, V7)
  line ep from V0 to V7
  # Q = Intersect(eb, ep)
  point Q = intersect eb ep
  # ln0 = Line(Q, v)
  line ln0 through Q parallel v
  # cB = Circle(O, 1.026)
  circle cB center(O) radius 1.026 * $unit
  # {Q1, Q2} = Intersect(cB, ln0)
  intersect Q1Q2_ cB ln0
  # {Q1, Q2} = Intersect(cB, ln0)
  point Q1 = Q1Q2_.cpt0
  # {Q1, Q2} = Intersect(cB, ln0)
  point Q2 = Q1Q2_.cpt1
  # bk = Segment(Q1, Q2)
  segment bk from Q1 to Q2
  # ln2 = Rotate(ln0, 72°, O)
  line ln2 = rotate ln0 by 72 around O
  # ln3 = Rotate(ln0, 108°, O)
  line ln3 = rotate ln0 by 108 around O
  # ln8 = Rotate(ln0, -72°, O)
  line ln8 = rotate ln0 by -72 around O
  # lnP0 = Line(V6, V9)
  line lnP0 from V6 to V9
  # lnP2 = Line(V1, V8)
  line lnP2 from V1 to V8
  # lnP3 = Line(V2, V9)
  line lnP3 from V2 to V9
  # W1 = Intersect(ln0, ln2)
  point W1 = intersect ln0 ln2
  # W2 = Intersect(ln0, lnP2)
  point W2 = intersect ln0 lnP2
  # W3 = Intersect(ln0, lnP3)
  point W3 = intersect ln0 lnP3
  # W4 = Intersect(lnP0, ln8)
  point W4 = intersect lnP0 ln8
  # W5 = Intersect(lnP0, ln3)
  point W5 = intersect lnP0 ln3
  # W6 = Intersect(lnP0, lnP2)
  point W6 = intersect lnP0 lnP2
  # T = ClosestPoint(ln0, O)
  point T = closest ln0 to O
  # cd = Circle(O, T)
  circle cd center(O) through T
  # wb1 = Segment(W1, W2)
  segment wb1 from W1 to W2
  # wb2 = Segment(W3, Q)
  segment wb2 from W3 to Q
  # wp1 = Segment(W4, W5)
  segment wp1 from W4 to W5
  # wp2 = Segment(W6, V9)
  segment wp2 from W6 to V9
  # wlow = Reflect({wb1, wb2, wp1, wp2}, h)
  point W1_wlow = reflect W1 across h
  # wlow = Reflect({wb1, wb2, wp1, wp2}, h)
  point W2_wlow = reflect W2 across h
  # wlow = Reflect({wb1, wb2, wp1, wp2}, h)
  point W3_wlow = reflect W3 across h
  # wlow = Reflect({wb1, wb2, wp1, wp2}, h)
  point Q_wlow = reflect Q across h
  # wlow = Reflect({wb1, wb2, wp1, wp2}, h)
  point W4_wlow = reflect W4 across h
  # wlow = Reflect({wb1, wb2, wp1, wp2}, h)
  point W5_wlow = reflect W5 across h
  # wlow = Reflect({wb1, wb2, wp1, wp2}, h)
  point W6_wlow = reflect W6 across h
  # wlow = Reflect({wb1, wb2, wp1, wp2}, h)
  point V9_wlow = reflect V9 across h
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # t=01:45  Oh = Reflect(O, O)
  point Oh = rotate O by 180 around O
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # pl4 = Line(V0, V3)
  line pl4 from V0 to V3
  # pl6 = Line(V5, V2)
  line pl6 from V5 to V2
  # L = Intersect(pl4, pl6)
  point L = intersect pl4 pl6
  # R = Reflect(L, v)
  point R = reflect L across v
  # mhc5 = Circle(O, 5)
  circle mhc5 center(O) radius 5 * $unit
  # mhc6 = Circle(O, 6)
  circle mhc6 center(O) radius 6 * $unit
  # mhN5 = Intersect(mhc5, v, V0)
  point mhN5 = intersect mhc5 v pick nearest V0
  # mhS5 = Intersect(mhc5, v, V5)
  point mhS5 = intersect mhc5 v pick nearest V5
  # mhN6 = Intersect(mhc6, v, V0)
  point mhN6 = intersect mhc6 v pick nearest V0
  # mhS6 = Intersect(mhc6, v, V5)
  point mhS6 = intersect mhc6 v pick nearest V5
  # mhE6 = Intersect(mhc6, h, E)
  point mhE6 = intersect mhc6 h pick nearest E
  # mhW6 = Intersect(mhc6, h, W)
  point mhW6 = intersect mhc6 h pick nearest W
  # mhy5 = Line(mhN5, h)
  line mhy5 through mhN5 parallel h
  # mhs5 = Line(mhS5, h)
  line mhs5 through mhS5 parallel h
  # mhy6 = Line(mhN6, h)
  line mhy6 through mhN6 parallel h
  # mhs6 = Line(mhS6, h)
  line mhs6 through mhS6 parallel h
  # mhx6 = Line(mhE6, v)
  line mhx6 through mhE6 parallel v
  # mhw6 = Line(mhW6, v)
  line mhw6 through mhW6 parallel v
  # mhR0 = Line(V0, R)
  line mhR0 from V0 to R
  # mhR5 = Line(V5, R)
  line mhR5 from V5 to R
  # A1 = Intersect(pl4, mhs5)
  point A1 = intersect pl4 mhs5
  # A2 = Intersect(pl4, mhy6)
  point A2 = intersect pl4 mhy6
  # F1 = Intersect(mhw6, mhy6)
  point F1 = intersect mhw6 mhy6
  # A3 = Intersect(pl6, mhy5)
  point A3 = intersect pl6 mhy5
  # A4 = Intersect(pl6, mhs6)
  point A4 = intersect pl6 mhs6
  # F2 = Intersect(mhw6, mhs6)
  point F2 = intersect mhw6 mhs6
  # A5 = Intersect(mhR5, mhy5)
  point A5 = intersect mhR5 mhy5
  # A6 = Intersect(mhR5, mhs6)
  point A6 = intersect mhR5 mhs6
  # F3 = Intersect(mhx6, mhs6)
  point F3 = intersect mhx6 mhs6
  # A7 = Intersect(mhR0, mhs5)
  point A7 = intersect mhR0 mhs5
  # A8 = Intersect(mhR0, mhy6)
  point A8 = intersect mhR0 mhy6
  # F4 = Intersect(mhx6, mhy6)
  point F4 = intersect mhx6 mhy6

pattern gBV_JTt3Kxk on gBV_JTt3Kxk_scaffold
  # ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # t=01:41  ring = Sequence(Rotate({wb1, wb2, wp1, wp2, wlow}, 36°*i, O), i, 0, 9)
  rotate 10 around O
    connect W1 -> W2
    connect W3 -> Q
    connect W4 -> W5
    connect W6 -> V9
    connect W1_wlow -> W2_wlow
    connect W3_wlow -> Q_wlow
    connect W4_wlow -> W5_wlow
    connect W6_wlow -> V9_wlow


  palette pal
    Slab = #9a9a9a
    Middle = #6b4e9b
    Kite = #e05a47
    Hex = #d4af37
    Star = #2f6db5
    Outer = #3a9a5b
    fill void where orbit == 0 color Middle
    fill void where orbit == 1 color Kite
    fill void where orbit == 2 color Hex
    fill void where orbit == 3 color Star
    fill void where orbit == 4 color Outer

coaster Coaster
  outline pattern
  inscribe gBV_JTt3Kxk
  base $height
  strap width $strap
  loose where orbit == 0 clearance $gap pack zipper spacing $spacing mark dots $dots_middle bottom $dots_bottom pins $pins_middle slide $slide_middle
  loose where orbit == 1 clearance $gap pack zipper spacing $spacing mark dots $dots_kite bottom $dots_bottom pins $pins_kite slide $slide_kite
  loose where orbit == 2 clearance $gap pack zipper spacing $spacing mark dots $dots_hex bottom $dots_bottom pins $pins_hex slide $slide_hex
  loose where orbit == 3 clearance $gap pack zipper spacing $spacing mark dots $dots_star bottom $dots_bottom pins $pins_star ring $pins_star_ring slide $slide_star
  loose where orbit == 4 clearance $gap pack zipper spacing $spacing mark dots $dots_outer bottom $dots_bottom pins $pins_outer slide $slide_outer
  split at $half studs $stud gap $stud_gap spacing $stud_spacing wall $stud_wall ring $stud_ring1 $stud_ring1_n turn $stud_ring1_turn ring $stud_ring2 $stud_ring2_n turn $stud_ring2_turn ring $stud_ring3 $stud_ring3_n turn $stud_ring3_turn ring $stud_ring4 $stud_ring4_n turn $stud_ring4_turn ring $stud_ring5 $stud_ring5_n turn $stud_ring5_turn boss $stud_boss hold flange $flange room $room halves $halves slope $slope pin $pin gap $pin_gap wall $pin_wall spacing $pin_spacing slide $slide gap $slide_gap height $slide_height extra $slide_slot_extra wedge $wedge from $wedge_from center $wedge_center
  # A 2 mm cap, not the 2.5 default: the undercut starts 0.6 mm up, level with the
  # letters' floor, so the id must sit under the rib the undercuts leave, and at
  # 2.5 mm no spot along a strap or at a junction has room.
  mark $id on bottom cap 2
